---
import Chapter from '../Chapter.astro';
import Figure from '../Figure.astro';
import FigBuildValidate from '../figures/FigBuildValidate.astro';

const questions = [
  {
    when: 'Before issuing',
    q: 'What does a cross-certificate enable before it is signed?',
    now: 'Constraints are audited only afterwards, when the bridge is already in production and every path it opens is live.',
  },
  {
    when: 'During the handshake',
    q: 'Which admissible path does the receiver prefer, under its own policy?',
    now: 'The vendor of each validation library decides, and it decides in the same way for every receiver that uses it.',
  },
  {
    when: 'After the decision',
    q: 'Why was a path accepted, or why was there none?',
    now: 'A trust store says yes or no, and nothing records the reason behind either answer.',
  },
];
---

<Chapter id="gap" num="3" kicker="The gap" title="Validating a path is specified. Choosing it is not.">
  <div class="prose">
    <p>
      RFC 5280 validates a chain in detail, but its first input is literally <em
        >“a prospective certification path”</em
      >: it does not search for one, and it does not prefer one. RFC 4158 describes how to build paths but is only informational, and SCVP lets a client ask a server for the best path while it <strong
        >never defines what makes a path better</strong
      >.
      <span class="sn"
        >RFC 5280 has been updated nine times since 2008, and the model follows the policy processing of RFC 9618,
        the <code class="x509">noRevAvail</code> extension of RFC 9608 and the CRL key-usage
        clarification of RFC 10007.</span
      >
    </p>
    <p>
      Meanwhile, constraints such as <code class="x509">nameConstraints</code> or <code class="x509">pathLenConstraint</code> are
      only checked once a chain has been walked, so path builders explore branches that were condemned
      before the walk started.
      <span class="sn sn--key"
        ><b>Key idea</b> A cross-certificate enables a space of names and policies below the CA it certifies, which is a much narrower thing than the CA itself.</span
      >
    </p>
  </div>

  <Figure num={3} class="figure--text" caption="Building and validating are specified by different documents, and nothing in between says which path to choose.">
    <FigBuildValidate />
  </Figure>

  <p class="key">Today nobody knows what a cross-certificate enables until it has been issued and is already in use.</p>

  <div class="questions grid-3">
    {
      questions.map((x, i) => (
        <div class="rule-top">
          <p class="label">{x.when}</p>
          <h3>{x.q}</h3>
          <p class="small">{x.now}</p>
        </div>
      ))
    }
  </div>
</Chapter>

<style>
  .questions {
    margin-top: 2.5rem;
  }
  /* The three moments are a sequence in time: before, during, after. */
  .questions .label {
    margin: 0 0 0.6rem;
    color: var(--brass-soft);
  }
  .questions h3 {
    font-size: 1.25rem;
  }
  .questions .small {
    margin: 0;
    color: var(--text-soft);
  }
</style>
